Sculpt the **Aegis**, a colossal six-legged walking fortress, as a static
{{voxel.width}}x{{voxel.height}}x{{voxel.depth}} smooth-surfaced model using only the `sn` meshing tool.

## How sculpting works

The only way to shape the surface is the `sn` binary already on your `PATH`. You
build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**.
Unlike a voxel tool that paints discrete cubes, `sn` maintains a **continuous
signed-distance field** — you **composite primitives** into it (adding material
with a sphere, box, ellipsoid, or cylinder; carving it away with another) and it
extracts a triangle surface from the field's zero level set. `sn` is the **Surface
Nets** extractor: it produces a **smooth, rounded, watertight, mid-fidelity**
surface with no sharp edges, at a fixed resolution; corners round by construction.

The field starts **empty**. Every primitive carries an **opaque** `#rrggbb` color —
there is no transparency. Each call only records the operation and renders nothing;
run `sn render` when you want to see the model — it extracts the surface to
`mesh.glb` and draws the preview PNG `model.png`. The recorded operations are your
submission. An empty log is already in place at
`{{workspace}}`; you produce the preview and mesh yourself by running `sn render`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, for example:

```
sn add-box --x 2 --y 2 --z 3 --width 4 --height 2 --depth 5 --color "#c69a4b"
sn add-sphere --x 2 --y 4 --z 3 --r 2 --color "#c69a4b" --blend 1
```

Run `sn --help` to list every operation and `sn <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Run
`sn render` to (re)draw `model.png`, then read it** to see your progress and decide
what to do next.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build the fortress up operation by operation until the model in `model.png` matches
the brief — a huge armored hull riding raised on six thick jointed
legs, a big main turret with a long forward cannon, a side turret out on each
sponson, and a sensor vane above, cleanly symmetric about its centerplane and built
as one continuous rounded surface. **Before you finish, run `sn render`** so the
geometry (`mesh.glb`) the result is built from is emitted — an unrendered model is
scored as empty. When you are satisfied, stop: the recorded operations are your
finished submission.
